# Cloudflare Pages routing rules — copied verbatim from public/ to dist/_redirects.
# Format: https://developers.cloudflare.com/pages/configuration/redirects/
#
# Planyr uses HASH routing (#/…, see src/app/route.js), so every real navigation lands
# on a file that already exists: "/" → index.html, "/sequence/" → its own index.html.
# The app route lives in the URL hash, which is never sent to the server. That means we
# deliberately add NO `/* /index.html 200` single-page-app catch-all here.
#
# WHY NO CATCH-ALL (B449): a `/* /index.html 200` rule answers EVERY unmatched path with
# index.html — including a content-hashed build chunk that a newer deploy has deleted
# (e.g. /assets/SitePlannerApp-<deadhash>.js). The browser then receives HTML (200,
# text/html) where it expected JavaScript and dies with the confusing
#   "Expected a JavaScript module script … but the server responded with a MIME type of
#    text/html"  /  "Failed to fetch dynamically imported module"
# crash, instead of a clean 404 the chunk-reload guard can recover from.
#
# Cloudflare serves real static files BEFORE consulting these rules, so the rule below
# only ever matches a /assets/ path with NO file behind it (a stale/deleted chunk) — it
# can never 404 a live asset. Force those to a real 404 so a stale-chunk load fails
# cleanly and src/app/chunkReload.js + ErrorBoundary can recover or surface honestly.
/assets/*  /404.html  404

# ── Clean-path ENTRANCES for the top-level workspace slugs (B576000/NEW-3) ─────────────────
#
# "No SPA catch-all" (above) means a bare top-level path like /food answers with 404.html —
# Planyr's own "That page isn't here" screen, a small STATIC file with none of the app's JS,
# not index.html silently mis-routing. The React app never even boots at that path, so no
# amount of in-app JavaScript can fix this — read src/app/route.js's own header before
# "fixing" this again in the app instead of here.
#
# These are the exact, narrow exception the no-catch-all rule above was protecting against a
# BLANKET version of: one 302 per KNOWN top-level workspace slug (never a wildcard), each
# straight to its hash form. A real HTTP redirect — not a 200 rewrite — so the browser lands
# on /#/<slug> before it ever requests page content; by the time index.html's own inline
# landing-page redirect (below) and src/app/route.js run, the hash is already the deep link,
# so NEITHER needed a single line changed for this to work. /assets/* is untouched — a
# content-hashed chunk still 404s cleanly, exactly as B449 requires.
#
# ⛔ KEEP THIS LIST IN SYNC WITH src/app/route.js's SLUG_BY_MODULE — test/foodModule.test.js's
# "clean-path entrances" suite fails the build if a slug is added there and not here (or here
# and not there). Both the bare and the trailing-slash form need their own line; Cloudflare's
# redirect matching is exact-path, not prefix, so /food does not also match /food/.
/site        /#/site       302
/site/       /#/site       302
/schedule    /#/schedule   302
/schedule/   /#/schedule   302
/markup      /#/markup     302
/markup/     /#/markup     302
/library     /#/library    302
/library/    /#/library    302
/notes       /#/notes      302
/notes/      /#/notes      302
/model       /#/model      302
/model/      /#/model      302
/food        /#/food       302
/food/       /#/food       302
